emit.prompt_template:
Current stage: make the change in your workspace.
Build artifact {{name}}. Brief: {{brief}}
Implementation plan: {{implementation_plan}}
Required check: {{check}}
Repair feedback: {{repair_feedback}}
Implement the smallest complete change. Read the relevant project files to make the change. Do not read framework internals, worker receipts, graph configuration or prior acceptance histories. Do not delegate or launch research, reviewer agents or an optimizer.
## Your workspace
Your workspace is a prepared clone of the last attempt, with its dependencies installed and its setup and generate commands run; its path is in the execution block above. Make the change there. `git diff HEAD` there shows what you changed since the last attempt; the run's base commit is {{base}}.
Run there, as often as you need, the tests your change touches: the test files you changed or added, and the tests that import the modules you changed. Run the brief's own example or CLI path there too. They run there as they run in the check.
Before you stop, run `refactory-targeted-tests` and fix what fails. It runs exactly what the self-check will run for your change. {{builder_tests}}
Do not run the whole test suite: the full check runs after you finish, beside the reviews.
If you wrap a command in `timeout`, give it about twice the time stated above; DSH workspace shell commands are killed after at most 10 minutes.
When you are done, stop: the workspace's diff is your answer. The runtime turns it into the next attempt, which the check and the reviews then judge. Return a short completion message.
The runtime runs the requested check and one focused QA review next. Preserve all requested behavior. Do not invoke downstream graph operations yourself. When the repair feedback names verifier findings, answer each in your final message: the finding, then fixed, or rebutted: <reason> <file:line>. The final message is saved as verifier-response.md.

focused_qa.prompt_template:
Perform one focused QA review of the requested change. Brief: {{brief}}
Artifact/package path: {{output_path}}
Emitted or changed files to review: {{review_files}}
Required check command and its runtime result: {{focused_check}}
Read those files and relevant existing checks; inspect other project source only when needed to understand a dependency or finding. Use the supplied runtime result as evidence for the check already executed. Run any additional checks the brief requires that this result does not cover. Check the requested behavior and obvious regressions; do not delegate or launch research, other reviewers, an optimizer or a full model evaluation. Honor checks explicitly requested in the brief, and state any that could not run. Do not modify the implementation during QA. Do not inspect framework internals, delivery receipts or prior acceptance histories to discover this stage's output contract; it is specified below.
Write focused-qa.json with exactly this shape: {"status":"pass" or "fail", "checks":[{"name":"...", "command":"... or source inspection", "status":"pass" or "fail" or "not_run", "evidence":"actual output or specific source finding"}], "findings":["unresolved defects"], "not_run":["checks outside this focused scope"], "repo_questions":[{"function":"<exactly as listed under Changed functions, e.g. pkg/mod.py::Class.method>", "duplicates":"...", "callers":"...", "shared_state":"..."}]}. At least one substantive check is required. A required check not run is a failure. Structural admission, runtime validation and this review are different evidence. After writing focused-qa.json, return a short completion message. The runtime reads that file, applies the repair bound and advances the graph; do not run the gate, generate extra reports or repeat completed checks for handoff.
Each entry in `findings` is a BLOCKING finding. End the first line of each BLOCKING finding with `[core]` when it breaks an ask in the brief, an acceptance criterion, a correction or constraint the person gave, or existing behaviour (a regression: name the test or file:line that shows it), and with `[non-core]` otherwise: extra tests, docs, edge cases beyond the asks, refactors and style. A finding with no tag counts as core. Non-core findings do not hold the change back; its pull request lists them as follow-ups. Write the blocking marker only at the start of a finding line, in its plain form: BLOCKING: <path>: <description>. Never wrap it in backticks, a code span, or quotation marks while explaining a check or describing the parser. When discussing the marker itself, call it "the blocking marker". Write every real finding as a plain line outside any fenced code block, because fenced blocks are not read as findings. When you show the format as an example rather than report a finding, put the example inside a fenced code block. A check of something non-core that fails ends its name with `[non-core]`.

The change: {{change}}
test-guardedness.json in the run directory, when there is one, says which new tests fail without the change.
{{review_instructions}}
Changed functions:
{{changed_functions}}


## Authoritative validation and intent baseline
{{runtime_evidence}}
Large requirement inputs (design, strategy, plan) are referenced by path and sha256 above rather than inlined. Read those files from the run directory and read authoring-evidence.json for the full validation receipt.
Prior validation/review diagnostics (resolve any evidence-format errors in your own report): {{repair_feedback}}
{{qa_gate}}
{{overwatch_review}}
The runtime has already executed canonical smoke/acceptance on this revision. Read its receipt and actual output files; do not repeat a full model/Task feed. Use targeted spot checks only for a concrete uncovered requirement. A numeric score, source inspection and requirement coverage are different evidence. The brief, accepted criteria/design and current user intent ledger are binding. Only explicit user changes or exclusions can alter them; an author, coordinator, reviewer or README cannot waive an ask. Missing required evidence is failed or unverified, never pending-but-passed. Do not modify the implementation. If a required tool/credential is unavailable, write `UNAVAILABLE:` with the dependency and exact unblock action; do not request recoding or unchanged retries.

Write intent-audit.json as {"requirements":[{"id":"baseline id", "requirement":"the concrete ask(s) checked", "status":"pass|fail|unverified|excluded", "clauses":[{"clause":"one distinct obligation", "sites":[{"site":"path:line", "tests":["test_file::test_name"], "scope":"connected"}], "evidence":[{"path":"file", "detail":"finding"}]}], "evidence":[{"path":"actual file path", "detail":"command/output or precise finding"}], "reason":"explanation", "user_change":"ledger id or brief, only for an explicit user exclusion", "user_quote":"exact text of that explicit exclusion"}]}. For every constraint or correction row, list each distinct clause — every separate obligation in the text — in the "clauses" array, each with its own evidence from the diff. Each clause must include a `sites` array: `"sites": [{"site": "path:line", "tests": ["test_file::test_name"], "scope": "connected"}]`. Each site names the exact file:line in the diff that builds this clause, the test(s) that reach it, and the evidence scope (local, connected, assembled, artifact). A multi-clause correction must have sites on each clause, not a single site on the row. Cite call sites alongside definition sites: a new helper's definition site needs a sibling call-site entry in an existing file. For each clause whose `sites` is empty, provide a `kind` field: "prohibition" (a "do not…" clause — requires a `pattern` field naming the forbidden text), "doc" (requires a `path` field naming a changed documentation file — .md, .rst, .txt or under docs/), "design" (requires a non-empty `heading` field naming a design.md heading that exists in the current file), or "test" (cite the code site plus the test — the code site, not the test file, is the site). A site inside a test file is not a code site. The `sites` value must be a JSON array (even if empty); omitting it or setting it to a non-array value is a structural failure.
Scope means (from code_reviewer.md category 8, quoted as written):
- local: a unit test exists that tests the stated outcome in isolation
- connected: a test exists that calls a real entry point crossing a module boundary, mocking only external I/O
- assembled: a smoke test or startup check verifies end-to-end behavior
- artifact: the artifact exists and has correct content
This repository's rules:
- a test that patches the project's own modules on the tested path is local, not connected;
- assembled means driving a mode's run() with no project-module patch on the path;
- artifact means reading the delivered output.
Omitting a clause that is in the text, or listing a clause with no evidence, is a structural failure. Cover EVERY baseline id in authoring-evidence.json, decomposing compound asks into separate rows with the same id when needed. Map every ask, accepted criterion, design obligation and current ledger instruction to actual current evidence. Evidence paths are relative to the run directory or absolute delivered file paths. Cite existing individual files, never directories, line-number suffixes or intent-audit.json itself. Write every probe fixture you create into the run directory, under a path namespaced to your own node (for example `scratch/<node id>/<name>`), never /tmp or any other outside location and never a peer's shared name: concurrent reviewers stage probes beside each other, so a flat shared name can be clobbered by a peer. The audit accepts only files inside the run directory or the delivered artifact, so a fixture kept elsewhere cannot be cited and its verification is lost. A pass needs a supporting file and specific evidence; an exclusion needs the actual explicit user change id, exact user_quote and reason. Report omissions as fail/unverified with precise reasons, even if other reviewers passed.
If authoring-evidence.json contains a non-empty "process_notes" list, write a "process_notes" key in intent-audit.json as [{"id":"ledger-N", "text":"the directive", "followed":true|false, "detail":"where in the run artifacts this was or was not followed"}]. Cite run artifacts other than intent-audit.json. Process notes are not baseline requirements and never block the gate; this section is informational so a person reviewing the run can see whether each directive was honoured.

